 ## Overview
-3D Grid Rasterization converts a triangular mesh into a discrete 3D occupancy grid (voxel array) by mapping mesh geometry to a fixed-resolution integer lattice [S1, unofficial, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of representing continuous surface geometry as a volume suitable for ray marching or other volume rendering techniques [G]. Unlike [Voxelization](12-voxelization.md) methods that may use ray casting or signed distance fields, this approach explicitly rasterizes triangles into grid cells, often snapping voxel centers to the grid [S2, unofficial, S3, unofficial]. It differs from [3D Voxel Grid Smoothing](833-3d-voxel-grid-smoothing.md) by producing raw occupancy without post-processing, and from [Signed Distance Field (SDF) Generation from Scanned Surfaces](854-signed-distance-field-sdf-generation-from-scanned-surfaces.md) by outputting binary or colored occupancy rather than distance values [S2, unofficial, S3, unofficial].
+3D Grid Rasterization converts a triangular mesh into a discrete 3D occupancy grid (voxel array) by mapping mesh geometry to a fixed-resolution integer lattice [S1, unofficial, S2, unofficial, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of representing continuous surface geometry as a volume suitable for ray marching or other volume rendering techniques [G]. Unlike [Voxelization](12-voxelization.md) methods that may use ray casting or signed distance fields, this approach explicitly rasterizes triangles into grid cells, often snapping voxel centers to the grid [S2, unofficial, S3, unofficial]. It differs from [3D Voxel Grid Smoothing](833-3d-voxel-grid-smoothing.md) by producing raw occupancy without post-processing, and from [Signed Distance Field (SDF) Generation from Scanned Surfaces](854-signed-distance-field-sdf-generation-from-scanned-surfaces.md) by outputting binary or colored occupancy rather than distance values [S2, unofficial, S3, unofficial].
 
